title: skgrepo-sets
pid: a6d75e71-a019-4fb2-8342-465f6fb9165f
body: |2-
  Skgrepo-sets are no longer declared; the retired =[[repo_sets]]=
  table is rejected with an error. The sets are exactly the
  *prefixes of the privacy ladder*: each owned skgrepo's name denotes
  the set consisting of itself and every more-public owned skgrepo; the
  most private owned skgrepo's set holds every owned skgrepo. Choosing
  a set is choosing "the most private owned skgrepo to make
  available." Apart from that choice, a connection shows or hides the
  foreign skgrepos, all of them at once; they are shown unless it hides
  them. The most private set with the foreign skgrepos shown is no
  skgrepo restriction. =default_repo_set= (top-level field) names the
  set when a client connects, with the foreign skgrepos shown; left
  out, it is the most private owned skgrepo. Naming a foreign skgrepo
  there is refused.

  Example:

  #+begin_src toml
  default_repo_set = "public"

  # The owned skgrepos in privacy order: most public first.
  # Foreign skgrepos may sit anywhere.
  [[repos]]
  name = "public"
  path = "me/public"      # owned => writable

  [[repos]]
  name = "Fred"
  path = "fred/public"
  foreign = true          # write-protected

  [[repos]]
  name = "private"
  path = "me/private"
  #+end_src

  Here the available skgrepo-sets are =public= = {public} and
  =private= = {public, private}, each with Fred's skgrepo added while
  foreign skgrepos are shown; =private= with them shown is no skgrepo
  restriction.
